    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muawiya-i.jpg" alt="Muawiya I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_coin_of_Muawiyah_I,_struck_at_the_Fasa_mint_in_Darabjird_(Fars).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LouisAragon (uploader), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Emirate of Córdoba was founded by Abd al-Rahman ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya</a>, a grandson of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Hisham, who survived the Abbasid massacre of his family after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in 750. He fled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where he sheltered among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> related to his mother, and sent his freedman Badr to win over the Umayyad clients settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Since its conquest in 711–716 the province had been ruled by governors and torn by conflict between Arab factions, Syrian troops who had arrived in 741 and Berbers. In 755 Abd al-Rahman landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almunecar">Almuñécar</a> on the south coast, and with the support of Umayyad clients, Yemeni Arab factions and part of the Syrian army he defeated the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-al-fihri">Yusuf al-Fihri</a> at the Battle of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-musara">Musara</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in May 756. He took the title of emir and ruled from Córdoba, acknowledging no authority from the Abbasid caliphs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. His first decades were spent fighting rebels and rivals: in 763 he destroyed an army raised in the name of the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur by al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ala-ibn-mughith">Ala ibn Mughith</a>, and he faced repeated revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley and among the Berbers before his death in 788.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mediterranean.jpg" alt="Mediterranean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">of the modification : Eric Gaba (Sting), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The society of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was diverse. A ruling Arab minority, divided between the descendants of the first conquerors and the Syrian army, which was settled in military districts in the south, lived alongside a larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> population, especially in the central plateau and the mountains, and a majority of native Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Over the ninth and tenth centuries growing numbers of Christians converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, becoming <em>muwalladun</em>, while those who remained Christian, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>, became increasingly Arabised. In 854 the Córdoban Christian writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-alvarus">Paul Alvarus</a> complained that young Christians preferred Arabic poetry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> learning. Christians and Jews were protected as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a></em>, paying a poll tax and keeping their own communal leaders and judges. The economy was based on irrigated agriculture in the river valleys of the south, grain, olives and vines, livestock and textile production, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Córdoba grew into a large city with markets supervised by a market inspector, palaces and suburbs. Trade linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and slaves, often from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, were imported for the army and the palace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law, introduced by scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-yahya-al-laythi">Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi</a>, became dominant, giving the jurists of Córdoba great social influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> commanded the strongest army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. It combined the Arab <em>jund</em>, the Syrian contingents who held land in return for service, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> troops and, increasingly, a standing force of paid soldiers and slave guards, which al-Hakam I enlarged and which made the ruler less dependent on the Arab elite. Frontier zones called the Upper, Middle and Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marches</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and Mérida, faced the Christian north and were held by local lords, among whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley grew especially powerful. In 778 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, invited by rebels in the north-east, marched on Zaragoza but had to withdraw, and his rearguard was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncesvalles</a>. The emirs sent regular summer campaigns against the north: under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba">Hisham I</a> armies sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> in 794 and 795 and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> in 793, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. In 844 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a> defeated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tablada">Tablada</a> and afterwards built a fleet and shipyards to guard the coasts. Much of the emirs’ military effort was directed inward, against rebellious Toledo, the marches and the rebels of the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-hafsun">Umar ibn Hafsun</a>, who held the mountain fortress of Bobastro from about 880, resisted the emirs for almost four decades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hakam-i.jpg" alt="Al-Hakam I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-i">Al-Hakam I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_al_hakam_i_17521.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismática Pliego, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was a monarchy in which power was passed within the Umayyad family, usually to a son chosen by the reigning emir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> did not claim the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> until 929, but they never recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. They built a centralised state modelled on Umayyad and Abbasid practice. A chief minister, the <em>hajib</em>, headed the government, assisted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and a chancery of secretaries; the chief qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> led the judiciary; and officials such as the chief of police and the market inspector governed the capital. The country was divided into provinces under governors, who collected taxes and led local forces, while the marches enjoyed wide autonomy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-i">Al-Hakam I</a> (796–822) faced the most serious opposition from his own subjects: according to tradition he had notables of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> massacred in 797, and in 818 he crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-arrabal">Revolt of the Arrabal</a>, a rising of the southern suburb of Córdoba, razed the quarter and expelled its inhabitants, some of whom settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and others who later seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a> (822–852) the administration became more elaborate, court ceremonial was developed, mints and textile workshops expanded, and the emirate exchanged embassies with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilos-emperor">Theophilos</a>. His reign is usually regarded as the high point of the emirate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mezquita_de_C%C3%B3rdoba_desde_el_aire_(C%C3%B3rdoba,_Espa%C3%B1a).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Toni Castillo Quero, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Emirate of Córdoba made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> an independent Islamic state with its own dynasty and capital, separate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and laid the foundations for the brilliant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in the tenth century. Its most famous monument, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>, was begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> around 785 and enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a> and later rulers; its forest of columns and double arches of alternating red brick and white stone became a model for Islamic architecture in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> brought Eastern culture to the West. The musician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziryab">Ziryab</a>, who arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 822, is credited by later tradition with introducing new fashions in music, dress, cuisine and manners at Córdoba, and the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ibn-firnas">Abbas ibn Firnas</a> experimented with glass-making and, reportedly, with flight. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law, established in this period, has dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a> in North-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> ever since. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabisation">Arabisation</a> of the Christian population created a distinctive Mozarabic culture whose emigrants carried Andalusi styles into the northern Christian kingdoms. The movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martyrs-of-cordoba">Martyrs of Córdoba</a>, in which some fifty Christians, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eulogius-of-cordoba">Eulogius of Córdoba</a>, were executed between 850 and 859 for insulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> or apostasy, remains one of the best-documented episodes of religious conflict in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii.jpg" alt="Abd al-Rahman III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_abd_al_rahman_iii_20386.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Numismática Pliego, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> ended not with collapse but with a transformation. Under Muhammad I (852–886) the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was challenged by the frontier lords and by revolts of <em>muwalladun</em>: Ibn Marwan al-Jilliqi founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> as an independent base in 875, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> was almost independent, and from about 880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-hafsun">Umar ibn Hafsun</a>, operating from Bobastro, became the leader of a great rebellion in the south. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mundhir-of-cordoba">al-Mundhir</a> (886–888) and Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> (888–912), Arab lords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-elvira">Elvira</a> also rebelled, and for a time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> controlled little more than Córdoba and its surroundings, though Abd Allah defeated Ibn Hafsun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poley">Poley</a> in 891. Ibn Hafsun’s conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> around 899 cost him Muslim support. In 912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>, grandson of Abd Allah, succeeded and began a methodical reconquest of the country: Seville submitted in 913, Ibn Hafsun died in 917, and Bobastro fell to the emir in 928. At the same time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, who had proclaimed a rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in 909, threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. In January 929 Abd al-Rahman III took the title of caliph and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>, and the Emirate of Córdoba became the Caliphate of Córdoba.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Emirate of Córdoba, founded by the last surviving Umayyad prince in 756, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> an independent Islamic state, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>, Arabised much of the Iberian population and created the institutions on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was raised in 929.</em></p>

